# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# Discovery requires checks BY JOB NAME, which is a guess at the PR gate and
# drifts silently: a renamed job, an added workflow, or any future check
# reporting under the github-actions app changes what is required without
# anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the intent reviewable.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE FIXES TODAY (#201). The settings page required two of the
# three checks ci.yml produces:
#
#     ["test-ubuntu-latest","validate against xfs_db + in-kernel XFS driver"]
#
# `test-darwin` ran on every pull request, reported, and could not block one.
# Nothing noticed, because nothing in the repository had an opinion about the
# list: a job was added, its check was never added to a settings page nobody
# re-reads, and the difference between "this job runs" and "this job gates"
# was invisible from the workflow. It is written down here now, so adding a
# job and forgetting to require it is a reviewable diff rather than a silence.
#
# WHY test-darwin GATES rather than advising. It builds the staticlib that
# DiskJockey's XFS extension links against, for aarch64-apple-darwin, and runs
# the suite on that target on an arm64 host. It is the only macOS/aarch64
# coverage in this repository, and the failures it exists to catch are exactly
# the ones no Linux job can see: the one target-conditional value in the C ABI
# (ENOTSUP is 45 on Darwin and 95 on Linux, so tests/capi.rs's ENOTSUP asserts
# were never compiled against their Darwin branch until this job existed),
# `c_char` signedness at the C ABI boundary, and pointer-width and alignment
# assumptions that x86_64 Linux happens to satisfy. A break there ships to the
# consumer that links the artifact, and `test-ubuntu-latest` stays green
# through all of it. A check that cannot block the merge it disagrees with is
# a check that reports rather than gates.
#
# Only these three: they are the only checks that run on `pull_request`.
# release.yml is `on: push: tags: ['v*']` and stress.yml is workflow_dispatch
# plus a weekly cron, so neither reports on a pull request at all, and a
# required check that never reports is a permanent block on every merge —
# GitHub reads "required and absent" as pending, with enforce_admins on and
# no failure to point at.
#
# No aggregate `ci-ok`-style job exists here yet, so the three real job names
# are declared instead. An always-run job that `needs:` all three and fails on
# failure/cancelled/skipped would be the better end state — it survives a
# rename, a matrix split or a path filter without stranding a merge — but
# adding one belongs in its own change, where the skipped/cancelled semantics
# can be reviewed on their own.
#
# Names must match the check-run names GitHub reports, which here are the
# `name:` of each job rather than its key: `test-${{ matrix.os }}` renders as
# `test-ubuntu-latest`, and the kernel gate's name is a sentence with spaces.
[checks]
	required = test-ubuntu-latest
	required = test-darwin
	required = validate against xfs_db + in-kernel XFS driver
